      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "We tested whether long-term administration of antioxidant vitamins C and E improves coronary and brachial artery endothelial function in patients with coronary artery disease (CAD)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Endothelial function is a sensitive indicator of vascular health. Oxidant stress and oxidized low-density lipoprotein (LDL) impair endothelial function by reducing nitric oxide bioavailability in the artery wall."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We randomly assigned 30 subjects with CAD to combined vitamin E (800 IU per day) and C (1000 mg per day) or to placebos in a double-blind trial. Coronary artery endothelial function was measured as the change in coronary artery diameter to acetylcholine infusions (n = 18 patients), and brachial artery endothelial function was assessed by flow-mediated dilation (n = 25 patients) at baseline and six months. Plasma markers of oxidant stress (oxidized LDL and autoantibodies) were also measured."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Plasma alpha-tocopherol (p < 0.001) and ascorbic acid (p < 0.02) increased with active therapy. Compared to placebo, there was no improvement in coronary and brachial endothelial vasomotor function over six months. Although vitamins C and E tended to reduce F2-isoprostanes (p = 0.065), they failed to alter oxidized LDL or autoantibodies to oxidized LDL."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Long-term oral vitamins C and E do not improve key mechanisms in the biology of atherosclerosis or endothelial dysfunction, or reduce LDL oxidation in vivo."
        }
      ]},
